#!/usr/bin/env bash
# eo_housekeeping -- read the standard, then bring this repository up to date.
#
# WHERE: run it at the root of YOUR repository. Everything it changes, it
#        changes here. Other checkouts are read and never written.
#
# WHAT IT HANDS AN ASSISTANT: two paragraphs. The first is nothing but
#        pointers -- what the ecosystem is, who the **president** is and so
#        where `docs/policy.md` and `docs/vision.md` are, this repository's own
#        README and docs, the checker, and the other checkouts on this machine.
#        The second is the goal: documentation made true, topics other tools
#        addressed to us answered, bugs in our own tooling fixed, a topic opened
#        for anything needing somebody else, and CI green at the end.
#
# **WHY IT POINTS RATHER THAN RESTATES, which is the whole design.** An earlier
#        version paraphrased the policy into five areas and shipped the
#        paraphrase at 1,749 words: a copy of somebody else's rules, installed
#        on a stranger's PATH, with nothing keeping it current. The policy has a
#        section about exactly that failure, so a command written to find stale
#        documentation had become the most stale-prone thing in the tree. It
#        also silently narrowed the job to whatever the paraphrase happened to
#        name. `tests/test_eo_cmd.py` holds it under five hundred words now.
#
# **IT PULLS BEFORE IT JUDGES.** Every question a run asks -- is this
#        documentation true of the tree, has anybody answered this topic, does
#        CI pass -- is asked of a checkout, and a checkout that is behind answers
#        all three wrong: work already done reads as outstanding, and work done
#        here comes back to somebody as a merge. So the goal paragraph opens on
#        switching to main, then `git pull --ff-only`, stopping on failure.
#        `--no-main` keeps the current branch. `--report` switches and pulls
#        too: a report of what is stale, computed from a stale checkout, is the
#        defect this command was sent to find.
#
# **THE DISCUSSION WORK RUNS UNDER A STANDING OVERRIDE, and the prompt says so
#        in as many words.** Every discussion file opens with a banner that is
#        the ecosystem's one build-failing rule: an agent answers a topic only
#        where a human instructed it and named the topic. A command run on a
#        habit names no topic, so this does not satisfy that rule -- it
#        overrides it, on the maintainer's standing instruction, recorded in
#        `docs/maintenance.md`. Saying so in the prompt is not ceremony: an
#        assistant that reads the banner without it stops there, correctly.
#
# THE PRESIDENT IS LOOKED UP, NEVER WRITTEN IN. The register says who holds the
#        office and lives in the office's tree, so finding the file is finding
#        the president -- and the prompt keeps naming the right one after the
#        office moves. The same pass gives the register's list of names, which
#        the neighbour list is filtered to: a checkout the register does not
#        name is somebody's working copy, and offering it as a tool to answer
#        is how a topic gets read out of the wrong tree.
#
set -euo pipefail

AGENT=claude
PRINT=0
SHOW=0
REPORT=0
MAIN=1

usage() {
  # --help is a request: stdout, exit 0. A bad flag is a mistake: stderr, 2.
  local out=1 code=0
  if [ "${1:-}" = "error" ]; then out=2 code=2; fi
  cat >&$out <<'USAGE'
eo_housekeeping -- read the standard the president keeps, then bring this
repository up to date.

Run it at the root of the repository being tidied. It brings the checkout up to
date on main first, because every question it asks is asked of a tree. Its goal
is then documentation that is true of the tree, the topics other tools of the
Eunoia ecosystem have addressed to you answered, bugs in your own tooling fixed,
and CI green.

usage: eo_housekeeping [--report] [--no-main] [--codex] [--print] [--show-prompt]

  --report        report only after switching to main and pulling
  --no-main       keep the current branch; still pull with --ff-only
  --codex         run codex instead of claude
  --print         run non-interactively and print the result
  --show-prompt   print the prompt and exit, running nothing

**It hands an assistant a place to read the rules, not the rules.** The
president holds the policy and the vision, and this looks up who that currently
is rather than naming one, so the prompt keeps pointing at the standard after
the office moves. A prompt that paraphrased the policy would be a copy of it
with nothing keeping it current -- which is the defect it goes looking for.

**The discussion work runs under a standing override**, recorded in this
repository's docs/maintenance.md. Every discussion file opens with the
ecosystem's one build-failing rule: a topic is answered only where a human
instructed it and named the topic. A command run on a habit names none, so the
prompt says plainly that it is overriding, which is also the only way an
assistant gets past the banner. It still writes in no tree but its own and
sends nothing anywhere.
USAGE
  exit $code
}

while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    --report) REPORT=1; shift ;;
    --no-main) MAIN=0; shift ;;
    --codex) AGENT=codex; shift ;;
    --claude) AGENT=claude; shift ;;
    --print) PRINT=1; shift ;;
    --show-prompt) SHOW=1; shift ;;
    -h|--help) usage ;;
    -*) echo "eo_housekeeping: unknown option $1" >&2; usage error ;;
    *) echo "eo_housekeeping: it takes no arguments -- what to look at is what" >&2
       echo "         the policy and this repository's own pages say, which is" >&2
       echo "         why they are read rather than summarised here" >&2
       usage error ;;
  esac
done

if ! US_ROOT=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null); then
  # --show-prompt still prints what it would send, from wherever you are.
  if [ "$SHOW" = 0 ]; then
    echo "eo_housekeeping: not in a git repository -- run this at the root of" >&2
    echo "         the repository being tidied. The work is left staged, which" >&2
    echo "         needs somewhere to stage it." >&2
    exit 2
  fi
  US_ROOT=$(pwd)
fi
US=$(basename "$US_ROOT")

# Where other checkouts might be. The same three places eo_respond looks, and
# for the same reason: no document here may name one machine, so
# nothing records them and each run finds them again.
IFS=: read -ra ROOTS <<< "${ANOIEU_REPOS:-}"
SEARCH=()
for r in "${ROOTS[@]}" "$(dirname "$US_ROOT")" "$HOME"; do
  [ -n "$r" ] && [ -d "$r" ] && SEARCH+=("$r")
done

FOUND=""
SEEN=" $US "
for r in "${SEARCH[@]}"; do
  for d in "$r"/*/; do
    [ -d "$d" ] || continue
    top=$(git -C "$d" rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null) || continue
    name=$(basename "$top")
    case "$SEEN" in *" $name "*) continue ;; esac
    SEEN="$SEEN$name "
    FOUND="$FOUND$(dirname "$top") $name"$'\n'
  done
done

# The office moves, so nothing here may name a president. The register says who
# holds it, and the register lives in the tree that holds the office -- so
# finding the file is finding the president, and reading it is confirming which
# one. A tree with no register found means the prompt asks for the standard a
# different way rather than guessing at a name.
PRES=""
PRES_DIR=""
for r in "${SEARCH[@]}"; do
  for d in "$r"/*/; do
    [ -f "$d/scripts/ecosystem/ecosystem.json" ] || continue
    PRES=$(awk '
      /^  "[^"]+": \{/ { n = $0; sub(/^  "/, "", n); sub(/": \{.*/, "", n) }
      /"status"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*"president"/ { print n; exit }
    ' "$d/scripts/ecosystem/ecosystem.json")
    if [ -n "$PRES" ]; then
      PRES_DIR=$(cd "$d" && pwd)
      REGISTER="$d/scripts/ecosystem/ecosystem.json"
      # Every name the register knows. A checkout it does not name is somebody's
      # working copy rather than a repository of this ecosystem, and offering it
      # as something to check a claim against is how a claim gets checked
      # against the wrong tree.
      KNOWN=" $(awk '
        /^  "[^"]+": \{/ { n = $0; sub(/^  "/, "", n); sub(/": \{.*/, "", n); printf "%s ", n }
      ' "$d/scripts/ecosystem/ecosystem.json") "
    fi
    break 2
  done
done

if [ -n "${KNOWN:-}" ]; then
  KEPT=""
  while read -r parent name; do
    [ -n "$name" ] || continue
    case "$KNOWN" in *" $name "*) KEPT="$KEPT$parent $name"$'\n' ;; esac
  done <<< "$FOUND"
  FOUND="$KEPT"
fi

# Child projects live in `tools/` and are this tree's own code and pages, so
# they are part of what a run tidies -- and the register is what says which are
# ours: a directory under `tools/` it does not name is not a child project. It
# also gives the branch a child's current work is on, which is why a child can be
# ours and yet not in this checkout. Only the disk is left to read when no
# register was found, and a repository with no children gets no sentence about
# children: a prompt pays for every word whether or not it says anything.
KIDS=""
if [ -n "${REGISTER:-}" ]; then
  KIDS=$(awk -v us="$US" '
    function val(s) { sub(/^[^:]*:[[:space:]]*"/, "", s)
                      sub(/",?[[:space:]]*$/, "", s); return s }
    /^  "[^"]+": \{/ { name = $0; sub(/^  "/, "", name); sub(/": \{.*/, "", name)
                       st = ""; par = ""; pa = ""; br = "" }
    /^    "status": "/ { st = val($0) }
    /^    "parent": "/ { par = val($0) }
    /^    "path": "/   { pa = val($0) }
    /^    "branch": "/ { br = val($0) }
    /^  \}/ { if (name != "" && st == "child" && par == us)
                printf "**%s** (%s%s), ", name, (pa == "" ? "tools/" name : pa),
                       (br == "" ? "" : ", whose work is on branch " br)
              name = "" }
  ' "$REGISTER")
elif [ -d "$US_ROOT/tools" ]; then
  for d in "$US_ROOT"/tools/*/; do
    [ -d "$d" ] || continue
    KIDS="$KIDS**$(basename "$d")** (tools/$(basename "$d")), "
  done
fi
KIDS=${KIDS%, }

CHILDREN=""
CHILDWORK=""
if [ -n "$KIDS" ]; then
  CHILDREN=" Its **child projects** are $KIDS — this tree's own code and pages
rather than repositories of their own."
  CHILDWORK=" **Those child projects are part of all three**: their pages true of
their own code, their own tooling working — and because a child opens no topic
and answers none, anything one of them needs from another tool is asked for here,
as ours."
fi

if [ -n "$PRES" ] && [ -f "$PRES_DIR/docs/policy.md" ]; then
  STANDARD="Its **president** is **$PRES**, at \`$PRES_DIR\`, and holds the standard:
\`docs/policy.md\`, which binds every repository here, and \`docs/vision.md\`, what
the arrangement is *for*."
elif [ -n "$PRES" ]; then
  STANDARD="Its **president** is **$PRES**, which holds the standard — \`docs/policy.md\`
and \`docs/vision.md\` — and is not checked out here; take them from the link this
repository's own README carries."
else
  STANDARD="Its **president** holds the standard — \`docs/policy.md\` and \`docs/vision.md\`
— and **nothing here says who that currently is**: take them from the link this
repository's own README carries, and say so in your report."
fi

if [ -n "$FOUND" ]; then
  NEIGHBOURS=$(printf '%s' "$FOUND" | sort | awk '
    { if ($1 != d) { if (d) printf " (in %s); ", d; d = $1; printf "%s", $2 }
      else printf ", %s", $2 }
    END { if (d) printf " (in %s)", d }')
  NEARBY="The other tools are checked out here — $NEIGHBOURS — and a topic addressed to us is one whose \`To:\` names **$US**, in that tool's \`docs/discussion.md\`."
else
  NEARBY="**No other tool of this ecosystem is checked out here**, so nothing can be read for topics addressed to us; say so in your report."
fi

# anoieu publishes the checker. Worth naming and no more than that: it decides
# the decidable rules, which is not what a person runs this for.
CHECKER=""
for r in "${SEARCH[@]}"; do
  if [ -f "$r/anoieu/scripts/policy_check.py" ]; then
    CHECKER="$r/anoieu/scripts/policy_check.py"; break
  fi
done
if [ -n "$CHECKER" ]; then
  MECHANICAL="anoieu publishes the policy checker, \`python3 $CHECKER --root $US_ROOT\`, which decides the rules that are decidable."
else
  MECHANICAL="anoieu publishes a policy checker for the rules that are decidable, and there is no checkout of it here."
fi

if [ "$MAIN" = 1 ]; then
  PULL="**Begin by ensuring this repository is on \`main\`**: inspect the current
branch, run \`git switch main\` if needed, and verify the branch is \`main\`.
If switching fails, say so and stop; do not discard, stash, or reset changes to
force it. **Then run \`git pull --ff-only\`.**"
  ALLOWED="switching to \`main\` and that pull"
else
  PULL="**Begin with \`git pull --ff-only\` on the current branch; do not switch branches.**"
  ALLOWED="that pull"
fi

if [ "$REPORT" = 1 ]; then
  CLOSE="**Change nothing** beyond $ALLOWED: write what you would have done to \`housekeeping.local.md\`
at the root instead, unstaged, and say whether CI passes here."
else
  CLOSE="**Ensure CI passes here as a final step.**"
fi

read -r -d '' PROMPT <<PROMPT_END || true
You are doing housekeeping in **$US**, at $US_ROOT — one repository of the
**Eunoia ecosystem**, a group of tools built around the Eunoia language and the
proofs cvc5 produces, sharing one repository policy and written mostly by AI
agents under light human supervision. $STANDARD Read both, and this repository's
own \`README.md\` and \`docs/\` for the conventions that are its own; this prompt
restates none of it. $MECHANICAL $NEARBY$CHILDREN

$PULL A checkout that is behind makes true documentation
read as stale and stale documentation read as true, and makes anything you write
here a merge for somebody else; if it will not fast-forward cleanly, say so and
stop rather than untangling somebody else's merge to get started. **Then, based
on those pages, your goal is to bring this repository's documentation up to
date, answer the discussion items other tools have raised with us, and fix any
bugs in our own tooling**, opening a topic in \`docs/discussion.md\` for any
request to another tool that arises.$CHILDWORK The maintainer instructs the discussion work
standing, which is the human instruction those files require; answer only what
names us, write in no tree but this one, and send nothing anywhere. $CLOSE
PROMPT_END

# Every paragraph above is assembled from fragments wrapped at whatever width
# they were written at, so the finished text is refilled rather than trusted.
# `fmt` is coreutils and is normally there; a prompt is still a prompt without
# it, so its absence loses the wrapping and nothing else.
wrapped() {
  if command -v fmt >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    printf '%s\n' "$PROMPT" | awk -v RS='' '
      NR > 1 { printf "\n" }
      { cmd = "fmt -w 78"; print | cmd; close(cmd) }' \
      | sed 's/\([.!?:;]\)  */\1 /g'
  else
    printf '%s\n' "$PROMPT"
  fi
}

if [ "$SHOW" = 1 ]; then wrapped; exit 0; fi
PROMPT=$(wrapped)

command -v "$AGENT" >/dev/null 2>&1 || {
  echo "eo_housekeeping: $AGENT is not on PATH" >&2; exit 127; }

WHAT="fixing what it can evidence"
[ "$REPORT" = 1 ] && WHAT="reporting after branch preparation and pull"
echo "-- eo_housekeeping: $US ($US_ROOT), $WHAT, via $AGENT" >&2
if [ -n "$PRES_DIR" ]; then
  echo "--   the standard: $PRES, at $PRES_DIR/docs" >&2
elif [ -n "$PRES" ]; then
  echo "--   the standard: $PRES, which is not checked out here" >&2
else
  echo "--   no register on this machine: the president is not named" >&2
fi
[ -n "$CHECKER" ] || echo "--   no checker on this machine: the decidable rules go unchecked" >&2

cd "$US_ROOT"
if [ "$AGENT" = codex ]; then
  if [ "$PRINT" = 1 ]; then exec codex exec "$PROMPT"; else exec codex "$PROMPT"; fi
else
  if [ "$PRINT" = 1 ]; then exec claude -p "$PROMPT"; else exec claude "$PROMPT"; fi
fi
